 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Image-space Gaussian Fine-tuning
-A fine-tuning process for volumetric data that accounts for different visual modes by generating multiple radiance fields using different transfer functions and optimizing the Gaussian set against rendered images [S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard fine-tuning by handling multi-modal volumes (e.g., medical scans) where a single radiance field cannot capture all features [S4, unofficial]. Choose this when dealing with volumes with distinct density ranges or material properties [S4, unofficial].
-- **Steps**:
-  1. Select a series of transfer functions $\{TF_h\}$, where each $TF_h$ is non-zero only within specific value intervals of the physical field $F$ [S4, unofficial].
-  2. Generate radiance fields $RF_h = TF_h(F)$ for each mode $h$ [S4, unofficial].
-  3. Render 64 images through volumetric integration for each radiance field to provide supervision data [S4, unofficial].
-  4. Compute image-space loss and backpropagate to update the Gaussian set $G_h$ [S4, unofficial].
-  5. After fine-tuning, Gaussians $G_h$ for each mode can be concatenated or separately rendered [S4, unofficial].
-- **Parameters**: 5 transfer functions are selected for each volume in practice [S4, unofficial].
+A fine-tuning process for volumetric data that accounts for different visual modes by generating multiple radiance fields using different transfer functions. It optimizes separate Gaussian sets against rendered images for each mode, allowing for global and local visibility control [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### Ada-GS
-A baseline method that trains vanilla 3D Gaussian Splatting directly on 2D stylized images [S5]. It sets itself apart from style transfer methods that operate on rendered images (like GS-Ada) by optimizing the 3D Gaussians directly against stylized supervision [S5]. Choose this when you need to apply a specific style to a 3D scene, but be aware that it requires training from scratch for every new style image, limiting practical usability [S5].
-- **Steps**:
-  1. Train vanilla 3DGS using 2D stylized views as ground truth supervision [S5].
-- **Comparison**: Preserves better details than GS-Ada but requires training from scratch for every new style image [S5].
+A baseline method for 3D Gaussian Splatting in Style that trains vanilla 3DGS directly on 2D stylized images. It preserves better details than GS-Ada but requires training from scratch for every new style image, limiting its practical usability [S5].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Fine-tuning is generally faster than training from scratch because it starts from a reasonable initialization [S3]. However, it still requires multiple iterations of differentiable rendering and backpropagation [S1, S3].
-- **Memory Complexity**: Memory usage depends on the number of Gaussians, which can grow during fine-tuning due to densification [S3].
-- **Performance**: Can achieve photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods, leveraging powerful pretrained weights [S3].
-- **Strengths**: High fidelity, adapts to scene complexity, works with sparse or noisy initialization [S1, S3].
-- **Limitations**: Requires per-scene optimization, which can be slow for large scenes; sensitive to initialization quality [S1, S3].
-- **When to use**: When you have a specific scene to reconstruct and a good initialization method (e.g., from a point cloud or feed-forward model) [S1, S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Fine-tuning is iterative and depends on the number of training views and iterations. Vanilla 3DGS fine-tuning typically takes several hours to converge on standard scenes [S3]. Generative fine-tuning (GPN) involves additional mesh reconstruction and NeRF optimization steps, increasing complexity [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians. Fine-tuning with densification can increase the number of primitives, requiring more memory. Pruning mechanisms help mitigate this [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Achieves high photorealism and can leverage pretrained priors for faster convergence [S3]. Allows for per-scene adaptation to specific constraints or styles [S1, S5].
+*   **Limitations:** Can be computationally expensive. Style transfer via 2D supervision may lack multi-view consistency [S5]. Generative methods may introduce artifacts if the prior is mismatched [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction is required for a specific scene, or when adapting a general model to a specific domain (e.g., style transfer, volumetric visualization) [S1, S3, S5].
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting builds on earlier work in NeRF fine-tuning and generative models [S1]. The concept of treating a reconstructed mesh or point cloud as a single-scene NeRF for optimization was introduced in GPN (Generative Point-based NeRF) [S1]. The use of feed-forward models to initialize 3D Gaussians for fine-tuning was later explored in EVolSplat [S3].
+Fine-tuning in 3D Gaussian Splatting evolved from the need to refine initial sparse reconstructions into dense, high-quality representations. Early methods relied on static SfM points, but fine-tuning allowed for dynamic densification and optimization [S3]. The concept of per-scene optimization was adapted from NeRF methods, where a global model is fine-tuned on a specific scene to improve quality [S1]. Multi-modal fine-tuning was introduced to handle complex volumetric data with varying visual modes [S4, unofficial]. Style transfer methods explored the application of fine-tuning to artistic domains, highlighting the trade-offs between 2D and 3D supervision [S5].
 
